The relationship between prompt length and accuracy remains unresolved. Accuracy was \amountSharedTwoAccuracy{}\% at a mean prompt length of \tierTwoTokens{} tokens and \amountSharedFourAccuracy{}\% at \tierFourTokens{} tokens. This paired change of \amountSharedTwoFourChange{} points comprised \amountSharedTwoFourWins{} wins, \amountSharedTwoFourLosses{} losses, and \amountSharedTwoFourTies{} ties over \amountSharedTwoFourDenominator{} cases from \amountSharedTwoFourFamilyCount{} families. At \tierEightTokens{} tokens, accuracy was \amountSharedEightAccuracy{}\%, with \amountSharedFourEightWins{} wins, \amountSharedFourEightLosses{} losses, and \amountSharedFourEightTies{} ties relative to the middle tier (Table~\ref{tab:amount}). Because the longest-tier intersection represents only \amountSharedEightCount{} probes from \amountSharedEightFamilyCount{} scenario families, these observations neither establish a benefit nor rule out degradation within the measured range.

